Example 2
Find the next two terms in each sequence.
a. {2, 4, 8, 16, 32, . . .}
b. {1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, . . .}
c. {1, −2, 2, −1, 3, 0, 4, . . .}
Solution
a. Each term is twice the previous term: 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128
b. Each term is the sum of the two previous terms: 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21
c. The pattern alternates between subtracting three and adding four: 1, −2, 2, −1, 3, 0, 4, 1, 5

A set is a collection of elements. Almost anything can be an element, for example, numbers, ordered pairs, variables, or geometric figures. The elements of a set are often listed in braces, { }. The set of whole numbers can be expressed this way:
{0, 1, 2, 3, …}
The ellipsis (…) indicates that the elements of the set continue in the same pattern without end. The set of integers can be expressed this way:
{…, −3, −2, −1, 0, 1, 2, 3, …}

To indicate an element of a set we use a symbol like a curved E.
3 ∈ {integers}
Read: 3 is an element of the set of integers
1/2 ∉ {integers}
Read: 1/2 is not an element of the set of integers

Example 2: Use set notation to indicate whether each number is an element of the set of rational numbers.
a. 2    b. 1/2    c. √2
Solution:
a. 2 ∈ {rational numbers}
b. 1/2 ∈ {rational numbers}
c. √2 ∉ {rational numbers}

